# EXAMPLE of /etc/pagespeed-optimizer/daemon.env -- secrets for the
# pagespeed-optimizer daemon, read by systemd (EnvironmentFile) before the
# service drops to the unprivileged `pagespeed` user.  Keeping them here
# instead of on the command line keeps them out of /proc/<pid>/cmdline
# (readable by any local user).
#
# This file is DOCUMENTATION.  The live file is created by the package's
# postinst with generated values and is deliberately NOT a conffile / not
# %config: a package-generated secret must never trigger an upgrade prompt or
# land in an .rpmnew.  A key that is already present is never rewritten, so
# your own values survive every upgrade; delete a key to have the next
# install generate a fresh one.
#
# Permissions: 0640 root:pagespeed.  Never world-readable.

# Bearer token for the HTTP management API.  Generated at
# install time.  The API itself stays OFF until you enable a transport in
# /etc/default/pagespeed-optimizer -- the credential is simply already there
# when you do, because a required token with no provisioning story is a
# requirement operators route around.
#
# The API refuses to start in any combination that would make it remotely
# reachable without this token.
# PAGESPEED_API_TOKEN=

# Token for PURGE over the management socket.  Generated at install time so
# cache invalidation works out of the box; without it PURGE answers
# "ERR no purge token configured".  The socket's 0660 group scope is the
# first factor and this token the second.
#   printf 'AUTH %s\nPURGE example.com https://example.com/x.css\n' "$TOKEN" \
#     | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/run/pagespeed-optimizer/notify.sock.mgmt
# PAGESPEED_PURGE_TOKEN=
